// Runtime/MapRuntime.maxon — what is genuinely a `Map`'s, and nothing else.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE HASH TABLE ITSELF IS `HashTableRuntime.maxon`'s.** A `Map with (Key, Value)` record IS a `Set`
// record with a value column appended — the first six slots (`count@0`, `capacity@8`, `keys@16`,
// `states@24`, `hashes@32`, `keyDrop@40`) are byte-identical, and `values@48` / `valueDrop@56` follow — so
// the probe, the grow, the insert, the remove and the teardown are ONE graph built for both containers, told
// which shape it is building. Read that file's header for the algorithm, the witness dispatch and the
// register-pressure rule; `__map_create`/`__map_grow`/`__map_upsert`/`__map_contains`/`__map_remove`/
// `__map_count`/`__map_decref` are all built there, and so is `__map_insert` (the shared insert graph under
// its refusing policy).
//
// What stays here is what a `Map` has that a `Set` does not:
//   • its `__map_*` entry-point names, its 64-byte record size, and its usage/witness predicates;
//   • `__map_get` — a lookup that misses has no value to answer with, and `MapError.keyNotFound` is what it
//     throws instead;
//   • the three composer answers that a throwing family owes (`isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee`,
//     `mapErrorThrows`, `mapSourceMethodName`) and the install-time guard that holds them to each other.
//
// Both columns are INDEPENDENTLY managed: a `Map with (String, int)` carries a `keyDrop@40` and a zero
// `valueDrop@56`, so its teardown walks the keys and never touches the values, and a `Map with (int, String)`
// does the opposite. That is why the shared drop helpers take the column as an argument rather than assuming
// the key's.

// The `Map` record: the shared `Set` prefix (48 bytes) plus `values@48` and `valueDrop@56` — eight 8-byte
// slots, 64 bytes. ⚠ **EXPORTED because `SignatureIndex.genericInstanceBoxSize` reads it** as a `Map`
// instance's box size, exactly as it reads `SetRecordBytes` for a set's.
export let MapRecordBytes = 64 as ByteCount

// The runtime entry points. `__`-prefixed (`isCompilerInternalCallee` routes their calls past the
// user-function checks — no file declares them) but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so `isMapRuntimeCallee` carries the
// `usesMap` (and, with it, `usesHeap`) bit rather than `isRuntimeCallee`'s raw-heap one — they `__mm_alloc`/
// `__mm_free` inside their Std bodies.
//
// ⚠ **`__map_upsert` IS INSERT-OR-REPLACE, and the name says so on purpose.** A `Set`'s `insert` is a no-op
// on a duplicate; a map's must REPLACE the stored value, dropping the old one. Two behaviours, two names,
// one shared graph with one branch (`HashTableRuntime.buildHtInsert`'s duplicate-key arm).
//
// ⭐ **`__map_insert` IS A THIRD BEHAVIOUR AT THAT SAME BRANCH, NOT A THIRD GRAPH.** It REFUSES a duplicate —
// the table is left exactly as it was and `MapError.keyAlreadyExists` is thrown — which is `specs/map.md`'s
// `insert(key, value) throws MapError`, and the one reason a `Map` has two insert entry points where a `Set`
// has one.
export let MapCreateName = b"__map_create"
export let MapUpsertName = b"__map_upsert"
export let MapInsertName = b"__map_insert"
export let MapContainsName = b"__map_contains"
export let MapGetName = b"__map_get"
export let MapRemoveName = b"__map_remove"
export let MapCountName = b"__map_count"
export let MapDecrefName = b"__map_decref"

// ⭐⭐ **`__map_entries(map, dest, pairBytes, valueOffset, keyClone, valueClone)` — EVERY LIVE ENTRY AS A
// `(Key, Value)` PAIR BOX, PUSHED INTO A CALLER-CREATED `Array with (Key, Value)`.** This is what a `Map`
// has instead of an element: `for pair in m` and `m.map(f)` both need a `(Key, Value)` VALUE, and a map
// stores no such thing — its keys and values live in two PARALLEL buffers, so the pair does not exist until
// something builds it. That is the whole of what this walk does, once, for both readers.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS WHY shv2 NEEDS NO `Map` CURSOR PROTOCOL, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE DEPARTURE FROM v1.** v1
// iterates a `Map` through `stdlib`'s `createIterator`, because its containers are Maxon types that can
// declare one. shv2's are hand-synthesized builtins and its `for` has a fixed set of forms, so a fifth one
// would have meant a new per-trip element ownership rule for a value that has to be ALLOCATED anyway — one
// pair box per entry, cursor or no cursor. Materializing the entries costs the array's own buffer on top of
// boxes that were always going to exist, and buys back the ARRAY form entire: the loop's counter, its live
// length read, its borrow classification and its drop are all the ones `for x in <array>` already has, and
// `map` is then literally `__managed_map` over the result.
//
// ⚠ **THE PAIR OWNS ITS COLUMNS — IT DOES NOT BORROW THEM.** The map keeps every key and value it holds
// (its `keyDrop@40`/`valueDrop@56` teardown walk destroys them), so a pair box holding the map's own pointers
// would be a box whose drop must not cascade — an ownership kind shv2 does not have, and a dangling read the
// moment the map outlives... or fails to outlive... the pairs. So a MANAGED column is CLONED into the box
// (`keyClone`/`valueClone`, the `(box) -> newBox` callees `SignatureIndex.managedFieldCloneStrategy` names,
// `0` for a trivial column) and the pair is an ordinary owned struct box: `dest`'s own `element_destroy@40`
// is the tuple's synthesized `__destruct_<T>`, so the array drops each pair and each pair drops its columns.
//
// ⚠ **THE ITERATION ORDER IS THE TABLE'S SLOT ORDER, AND THAT IS UNSPECIFIED BY DESIGN** — the same claim
// `__set_map` makes for the same reason: a hash table is unordered, and inventing an order here would promise
// one the language does not.
export let MapEntriesName = b"__map_entries"

// The pair box's KEY slot. A tuple is a synthesized struct whose element `_0` is its first field, and a
// struct's first field sits at displacement 0 from the payload pointer `__mm_alloc` answers
// (`Project.StructLayout.offsetOfField`) — so this one is a property of the layout builder rather than of
// any particular pair, and it is the reason only the VALUE slot's offset has to be passed in.
let PairKeySlotOffset = 0 as ParsedInt

// `__map_grow` is INTERNAL — called only by the two insert entry points when the load factor is crossed (and
// on the first insert, from capacity 0). It reuses the cached hashes, so it needs NO witnesses; kept out of
// the public name set so no parser path dispatches to it.
let MapGrowName = b"__map_grow"

// The `__map_` prefix identifying EVERY map runtime entry point — the one test `recordCallUsage` reads to set
// `usesMap` (and `usesHeap`).
let MapRuntimePrefix = b"__map_"

export function isMapRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: MapRuntimePrefix)
end 'isMapRuntimeCallee'

// Is `callee` a map runtime function that DISPATCHES THROUGH THE KEY'S WITNESSES — `upsert`/`insert`/
// `contains`/`get`/`remove`, the five that hash the key and compare it. `create`/`count`/`decref`/`grow`
// touch no key VALUE (the grow reuses the cached hashes), so they carry no witnesses.
// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerRuntimeCall` reads this to decide whether to append the two hidden witness-table
// addresses to a map runtime call — the `Set` twin, and it threads the KEY's tables (type argument
// `HashTableKeyArgPosition`), never the value's.
export function isMapWitnessCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(MapUpsertName) or callee.equals(MapInsertName) or callee.equals(MapContainsName) or callee.equals(MapGetName) or callee.equals(MapRemoveName)
end 'isMapWitnessCallee'

// The THROWING map runtime callees — `__map_get` and `__map_insert`. Each returns via the dual-register
// `errorReturn` ABI and can throw a `MapError` case, so a call to one MUST become a `tryCall` (it is under a
// `try`, which `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` converts). The map-side twin of
// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee`: the families are composed by
// `GtRuntime.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`, read at the ONE call site in the parser (E3055 accept) and the ONE in
// SemanticCheck (E3057 reject), so the accept and the reject cannot name different sets.
//
// ⚠ `upsert` does NOT throw, and that is a decision with a case behind it rather than an omission: it
// REPLACES a duplicate's value, so there is no failure for it to report. `insert` refuses one and therefore
// does throw — which is the whole difference between the two entry points and why both exist.
export function isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(MapGetName) or callee.equals(MapInsertName)
end 'isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **THE `throws MapError` STAMP `__map_get` AND `__map_insert` CARRY, AND WHAT A CALLER'S `otherwise (e)`
// BINDING IS TYPED FROM** — `arrayErrorThrows`'s twin, exported for the same reason: `GtRuntime.runtimeThrowsClause` pairs
// each family's predicate with its clause speller, and two hand-written spellings of one enum's name is what
// that composer exists to prevent. The name is `SignatureIndex.MapErrorTypeName` — the BARE `MapError`, which
// that constant explains at length (a user function propagating a `map.get` failure writes `throws MapError`
// in its own source, so a reserved `__`-spelling would meet E3059 against itself).
export function mapErrorThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(MapErrorTypeName))
end 'mapErrorThrows'

// ⭐ **WHAT THE USER ACTUALLY WROTE (D12)** — this family's `arrayAccessorSourceMethodName`, read through the
// one composer (`GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`) so that an E3057 on a missing `try`, and an E3059 on a
// valueless one, quote the method the author typed rather than a `__` symbol E2051 forbids them to type.
//
// ⚠ **THE SECOND ARM ARRIVED EXACTLY WHERE THIS HEADER SAID IT WOULD.** It read *"one name TODAY for a
// reason that is visibly temporary — `insert` (refusing a duplicate with `MapError.keyAlreadyExists`) is
// already declared in the enum"*, and the map was built with its panic arm rather than folded away for that
// reason. Both arms answer with the parser's own method constants (`SignatureIndex.GetMethod` /
// `InsertMethod`), the byte strings its dispatch matched, never a second `b"get"`.
export function mapSourceMethodName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if callee.equals(MapGetName) 'get'
		return GetMethod
	end 'get'
	if callee.equals(MapInsertName) 'insert'
		return InsertMethod
	end 'insert'
	panic("mapSourceMethodName: no source-method arm for '{String.from(callee)}'. EITHER it is not a throwing `Map` runtime callee — this map is asked ONLY behind `isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee`, so nothing else can reach an E3057 — OR that predicate gained a name and this map did not, in which case add an arm answering the parser's own method constant. `installMapRuntime`'s drift guard asks this for every throwing `__map_` builder it installs, so the second case fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`")
end 'mapSourceMethodName'

// `MapError`'s two ORDINALS — `Project.builtinMapErrorEnum`'s cases in declaration order, and the wire
// format each failure edge writes into the error-flag register (`__map_get`'s miss, `__map_insert`'s
// duplicate). Each flag is DERIVED from the one `MaxonDialect.ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` rather than written as a
// bare number, so the runtime encode cannot drift from the parser's decode. Both are held to the declaration
// on every map-using compile by `verifyMapErrorOrdinals`.
let MapKeyNotFoundOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
let MapKeyNotFoundFlag = MapKeyNotFoundOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let MapKeyAlreadyExistsOrdinal = 1 as ParsedInt
let MapKeyAlreadyExistsFlag = MapKeyAlreadyExistsOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// The name this family's ordinals are transcribed under, for `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s diagnostic.
let MapErrorOrdinalInstaller = b"the Map runtime (MapError)"

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD FOR `MapError`** — the sixth family on `Project.BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`, and
// it exists for that mechanism's stated reason: this file transcribes `keyNotFound`'s ordinal as a literal
// and `stdlib/Map.maxon:6` declares it by POSITION, so a case inserted before it would silently retarget
// every `otherwise (e)` arm in the program — no crash, no wrong exit code, just a handler naming the wrong
// case.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS CHECKING A LAYOUT THE PROGRAM DID NOT USE, AND THE LINE ABOVE SAID WHY WITHOUT NOTICING.** This
// read *"`MapError` is a compiler-SYNTHESIZED declaration (shv2 does not load `stdlib/Map.maxon`), so it takes
// `overSynthesized`, as `ArrayError` and `StringError` do."* W41 listed that module — the very rung that made
// `Map` the corpus's — so the parsed declaration has been displacing the seed on the same bare registry key
// ever since, while this guard went on rebuilding the seed and comparing it with itself. Retired here with
// `ArrayError`'s and `StringError`'s; see the ⛔⛔ block in `Project.maxon` for the measurement that caught it.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH CASES ARE CLAIMED NOW, and only one was.** The note here read *"`keyAlreadyExists` is declared but
// nothing emits it yet, and claiming an ordinal no runtime writes would be a dead claim"* — true while
// `upsert` was the only insert entry point. `__map_insert`'s duplicate arm writes it, so the claim is live
// and the ordinal is load-bearing: a case inserted between the two would silently retarget every
// `otherwise (e)` arm that matches `keyAlreadyExists` (`map.md:insert.duplicate-error-binding` is exactly
// that program) — no crash, no wrong exit code, just a handler naming the wrong case.
export function verifyMapErrorOrdinals(project Project)
	match declaredBuiltinErrorEnum(project, typeName: MapErrorTypeName, installer: MapErrorOrdinalInstaller) 'declaration'
		notDeclared then return
		found(declaration) then claimMapErrorOrdinals(project, declaration: declaration)
	end 'declaration'
end 'verifyMapErrorOrdinals'

function claimMapErrorOrdinals(project Project, declaration EnumLayout)
	var mapErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overDeclared(project, declaration: declaration, installer: MapErrorOrdinalInstaller)
	mapErrors.claim(MapErrorKeyNotFoundCaseName, ordinal: MapKeyNotFoundOrdinal)
	mapErrors.claim(MapErrorKeyAlreadyExistsCaseName, ordinal: MapKeyAlreadyExistsOrdinal)
end 'claimMapErrorOrdinals'

// Install the map runtime into the lowered Std module — ALL of it, when the program touches any map
// (`usesMap`). Golden-neutral: `__`-prefixed functions are excluded from the spec goldens
// (`isRuntimeFunction`), so an all-or-nothing install costs nothing in the fragments and needs no per-helper
// dependency tracking; the helpers call each other (`upsert`→`grow`→`__mm_alloc`) so a dependency graph would
// only re-derive the closure this install already is. Appended AFTER the pipeline (like `installSetRuntime`),
// so they are never re-optimized.
//
// ⭐ The seven shared entries come from `installHashTable`, told THIS container's names and shape — the one
// place a `Map`'s hash-table identity is stated. What is built after it is what this container does not
// share: `get`, `entries`, and the SECOND insert entry point.
//
// ⭐ **`__map_insert` IS `buildHtInsert` AGAIN, TOLD TO REFUSE — not a second insert graph.** The family
// names ONE insert (`__map_upsert`, the `keep` policy every container has); this is the extra door a `Map`
// has, so it is built here beside `get` rather than folded into a shape a `Set` would have to answer for.
// The refusal's flag and its `throws` clause travel together in the policy for `HtDuplicatePolicy`'s stated
// reason, and the drift guard below then checks the stamp against `isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee`.
export function installMapRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if not usage.usesMap 'noMap'
		return
	end 'noMap'
	let family = HashTableFamily.create(MapCreateName, growName: MapGrowName, insertName: MapUpsertName, containsName: MapContainsName, removeName: MapRemoveName, countName: MapCountName, decrefName: MapDecrefName, recordBytes: MapRecordBytes, carriesValues: true)
	installHashTable(module, family: family)
	buildHtInsert(module, family: family, name: MapInsertName, onDuplicate: HtDuplicatePolicy.refuse(MapKeyAlreadyExistsFlag, throwsClause: mapErrorThrows()))
	buildMapGet(module)
	buildMapEntries(module)

	// ⭐ **THE THREE LISTS, HELD TO EACH OTHER AT INSTALL** — `installManagedDirectoryRuntime`'s guard, one
	// family over. `isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee` names a set, the builders stamp a `throws` clause on a set, and
	// `mapSourceMethodName` maps a set; nothing in the language ties the three. Checked in BOTH directions for
	// their stated reason — a stamp without a name is a `try` that silently stops being required (and, on
	// wasm, a signature the call site disagrees with), and a name without a stamp is an E3055 on a call that
	// cannot fail. The missing source-method arm is that map's own panic, fired at INSTALL of any program that
	// uses a map — which the spec suite does on every run — instead of in front of a user who merely forgot a
	// `try`. Reasoning lives once, on `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`.
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not isMapRuntimeCallee(func.name) 'notOurs'
			continue
		end 'notOurs'
		let named = isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee(func.name)
		let stamped = throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName)
		if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
			panic("installMapRuntime: '{String.from(func.name)}' stamps a `throws` clause but isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee does NOT name it — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted")
		end 'stampedNotNamed'
		if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
			panic("installMapRuntime: isThrowingMapRuntimeCallee names '{String.from(func.name)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted")
		end 'namedNotStamped'

		if named 'hasSourceMethod'
			requireQuotableSourceSpelling(mapSourceMethodName(func.name), callee: func.name)
		end 'hasSourceMethod'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'installMapRuntime'

// ⭐ `__map_get(map, key, hashWitness, eqWitness) -> errorReturn(value, flag)` — probe for `key`; a matching
// Occupied slot answers its `values@48` slot with `NoErrorFlag`, and a miss throws `MapError.keyNotFound`.
// The `Map`'s READ failure, beside `__map_insert`'s write failure: the two reasons `Map` has an error enum.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE RETURNED VALUE IS A BORROW — the map keeps owning it.** This is `__managed_get`'s contract exactly: no
// clone, no refcount bump, no transfer. A managed value handed out here is still the map's to drop at
// teardown (through `valueDrop@56`), so a caller that stored it as an owner would double-free, and one that
// dropped it would leave the map holding freed memory. The parser's borrow classification of the result is
// what enforces that, and it must classify `__map_get` exactly as it classifies `__managed_get`.
//
// ⚠ **THE EMPTY-TABLE EDGE AND THE MISS EDGE ARE ONE BLOCK.** A capacity-0 map contains nothing, so its
// answer IS the miss's answer — the same `errorReturn(0, keyNotFound)` — and a second block spelling it would
// be one exit written twice.
function buildMapGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(MapGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 4, paramTypes: fourI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: mapErrorThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(4)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var probe = module.addBlock(func, label: b"probe")
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"found")
	var notFound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notfound")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 4)
	let map = 0 as ValueId
	let key = 1 as ValueId
	let hashWitness = 2 as ValueId
	let eqWitness = 3 as ValueId

	emitHtEmptyTableCheck(module, entry: entry, table: map, emptyBlock: notFound, probeBlock: probe, ids: ids)

	let matchedIndex = emitHtLookupProbe(module, func: func, cur: probe, table: map, key: key, hashWitness: hashWitness, eqWitness: eqWitness, foundBlock: found, notFoundBlock: notFound, ids: ids)

	// found: answer the matched slot's value. `matchedIndex` is the probe header's live index — the header
	// dominates `found` — so it names the slot the probe stopped on.
	let value = emitHtRecordSlotLoad(module, block: found, table: map, bufferOffset: HtValuesOffset, index: matchedIndex, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: found, value: value, ids: ids)

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: notFound, flag: MapKeyNotFoundFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildMapGet'

// `__map_entries(map, dest, pairBytes, valueOffset, keyClone, valueClone)` — see `MapEntriesName`. `map` is
// BORROWED (its columns are read and CLONED, never moved out and never nulled); `dest` is the caller-created
// `Array with (Key, Value)` the pair boxes are pushed into, and it becomes their sole owner.
//
// The pair box is a plain `__mm_alloc(pairBytes, 0)` — a struct box's own MM-header destructor is 0 because
// a tuple's drop cascade is reached through the ARRAY's `element_destroy@40` stamp, exactly as
// `Parser.emitBoxAlloc` allocates a `Self{…}`/tuple literal's box. Both offsets are the caller's to state
// (`StructLayout.offsetOfField`): a tuple's slots are not unconditionally two machine words, so a runtime
// that assumed `0`/`8` would silently mis-place the value column of a narrow-slotted pair.
function buildMapEntries(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(MapEntriesName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 6, paramTypes: sixI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(6)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var work = module.addBlock(func, label: b"body")
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"step")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 6)
	let map = 0 as ValueId
	let dest = 1 as ValueId
	let pairBytes = 2 as ValueId
	let valueOffset = 3 as ValueId
	let keyClone = 4 as ValueId
	let valueClone = 5 as ValueId

	let slot = emitHtOccupiedSlotWalk(module, func: func, cur: entry, table: map, work: work, step: step, done: done, ids: ids)

	let key = emitHtRecordSlotLoad(module, block: work, table: map, bufferOffset: HtKeysOffset, index: slot, ids: ids)
	let value = emitHtRecordSlotLoad(module, block: work, table: map, bufferOffset: HtValuesOffset, index: slot, ids: ids)
	let boxDtor = emitConst(module, block: work, value: TrivialDestructor, ids: ids)
	let box = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(work, op: StdOp.call(box, callee: MmAllocName, args: twoArgs(pairBytes, second: boxDtor)))
	let keyFieldOffset = emitConst(module, block: work, value: PairKeySlotOffset, ids: ids)

	// The two columns are placed in field order, each through the SAME gate, and the block is threaded from
	// one to the next: the key's placement ends in the block the value's begins in. `box` is defined in `work`,
	// which dominates both, so neither gate needs to carry it.
	var afterKey = emitMapPairColumnPlace(module, func: func, cur: work, box: box, fieldOffset: keyFieldOffset, column: key, clone: keyClone, ids: ids)
	var afterValue = emitMapPairColumnPlace(module, func: func, cur: afterKey, box: box, fieldOffset: valueOffset, column: value, clone: valueClone, ids: ids)

	let pushed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterValue, op: StdOp.call(pushed, callee: ManagedPushName, args: twoArgs(dest, second: box)))
	module.setTerminator(afterValue, op: StdOp.branch(step.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildMapEntries'

// ⭐ **PLACE ONE COLUMN'S WORD INTO THE PAIR BOX, TAKING A SECOND OWNER OF IT IFF THE COLUMN IS MANAGED** —
// `HashTableRuntime.emitHtDropIfManaged`'s dual, gated the same way and for the same reason: the stamp
// (`clone`) is `0` for a trivial column, so a `callIndirect(0)` is what a missing gate would emit. A managed
// column's `(box) -> newBox` clone answers a FRESH record the pair owns outright; a trivial column's word
// rides inline and is stored as it was read.
//
// It is written as TWO arms rather than "store, then overwrite when managed" so the box holds a pointer it
// does not own at no point at all — a window nothing could observe today, but one whose safety would have to
// be re-argued by anyone who later put an allocation between the two stores. Threads `cur` (sets its
// terminator) and returns the block both paths converge on.
function emitMapPairColumnPlace(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, box ValueId, fieldOffset ValueId, column ValueId, clone ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var doClone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mpeclone")
	var asRead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mperaw")
	let cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mpeplaced")

	// "No callee for this column" is ONE wire value, and it is `TrivialDestructor` — the very constant the
	// parser's `columnCloneValue` emits on the write side, read from its one declaration rather than
	// respelled here (`buildMapEntries` above already reads it for the pair box's own `element_destroy`).
	// A cloner and a destructor are different facts sharing an encoding, and a second name for the encoding
	// is what lets one side move: change it to a non-zero sentinel and this gate would keep testing 0 —
	// a `callIndirect(0)` on a trivial column, or a skipped clone on a managed one.
	let zeroClone = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: TrivialDestructor, ids: ids)
	let hasClone = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: clone, b: zeroClone, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasClone, thenBlockId: doClone.id, elseBlockId: asRead.id))

	let cloned = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doClone, op: StdOp.callIndirect(cloned, calleeReg: clone, resultType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), argFloatMask: 0, args: argsOf(column)))
	emitMapPairSlotStore(module, block: doClone, box: box, fieldOffset: fieldOffset, value: cloned, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doClone, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	emitMapPairSlotStore(module, block: asRead, box: box, fieldOffset: fieldOffset, value: column, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(asRead, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	return cont
end 'emitMapPairColumnPlace'

// Store one word at `box + fieldOffset`, where the displacement is a RUN-TIME value rather than the
// compile-time `ByteOffset` `emitStoreField` takes: this runtime function is built once for the whole
// program, so a pair's slot offsets arrive as arguments and the address arithmetic is explicit.
function emitMapPairSlotStore(module StdModule, block IrBlock, box ValueId, fieldOffset ValueId, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let addr = emitBin(module, block: block, a: box, b: fieldOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(addr, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, valueId: value, valueType: StdType.i64))
end 'emitMapPairSlotStore'
